1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:15,437 --> 00:00:19,070
Another kind of resource is a layout file.

3
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:21,700
Layout files specify what
the user interface for

4
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:25,180
some part of your
application will look like.

5
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:27,820
And again, these files are written in XML.

6
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:32,030
Although some tools will allow you
to create the layout visually.

7
00:00:32,030 --> 00:00:35,090
And then those tools will
generate the XML for you.

8
00:00:35,090 --> 00:00:36,260
In fact, Eclipse will do that.

9
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:43,980
Layout files are typically stored in the
res/layout directory of your application.

10
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:50,368
And you can access the layout
in Java as R.layout.layout_name.

11
00:00:50,368 --> 00:00:58,200
And you can access that layout in other
resource files as &layout/ layout name.

12
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:05,230
Now just like string files whose use
depends on your default language,

13
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:09,250
Android allows you to create multiple
layout files, and then Android can

14
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:14,790
choose from those files at runtime
based on your device's configuration.

15
00:01:14,790 --> 00:01:15,926
Let's look at an example of that.

16
00:01:22,555 --> 00:01:27,600
So let's look at the main.xml file
that's in the res/layout directory.

17
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:32,870
This file specifies that
the layout is composed of

18
00:01:32,870 --> 00:01:38,610
something called the RelativeLayout, one
that when we talk about user interfaces.

19
00:01:38,610 --> 00:01:44,130
And inside the relative layout, there's
another element called an edit text.

20
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:48,500
That's the box that you use for
entering the postal address.

21
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:52,350
There's also a button, that's
the button that was labeled show map.

22
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:59,380
Eclipse can also show what it thinks
this will all look like at runtime.

23
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:02,380
And you can see that by clicking
on the graphical layout tab.

24
00:02:04,665 --> 00:02:09,325
Now once you're there, you can also
click on the individual elements, and

25
00:02:09,325 --> 00:02:13,439
Eclipse will show you more detailed
information about their layout properties.

26
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:20,209
Map location also has another file,
also called main.xml but

27
00:02:20,209 --> 00:02:24,190
this one is in
the res/layout-land directory.

28
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:29,220
If map location is running with
a device in landscape mode,

29
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:33,210
this file is used instead of
the one that we saw before.

30
00:02:34,490 --> 00:02:39,160
Now as you can see, this file uses the
same elements, a relative layout, an edit

31
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:45,030
text, and a button, but I've changed their
positioning slightly so that the elements

32
00:02:45,030 --> 00:02:49,960
all appear on a single line, which I
think looks better in landscape mode.

33
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:53,170
And again, you can play around with
Eclipse to get more details about

34
00:02:53,170 --> 00:02:56,750
the layout properties of
each individual element.

35
00:02:56,750 --> 00:02:58,320
Now let's run the application.

36
00:03:00,510 --> 00:03:03,920
The phone on the left is
running in portrait mode, so

37
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:07,410
it uses the default main.xml file.

38
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:10,970
The phone on the right, however,
is running in landscape mode.

39
00:03:10,970 --> 00:03:16,744
So it will use the main.xml
file that's in res/layout-land.

40
00:03:18,020 --> 00:03:22,300
And as you can see the elements are
actually laid out differently depending on

41
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:23,650
the orientation of the device.

42
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:29,780
Now I've mentioned a few times now
that resources can be accessed in

43
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:33,300
Java as R.something or another.

44
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:40,200
Well, to do this Android generates
a class called R, from your application.

45
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:44,820
And you can then access the fields of
this R class to get to those strings and

46
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:49,500
layouts and other resources that
you defined in the XML files.

47
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:53,241
Let's take a look at an,
at an actual R.Java file.

48
00:04:02,056 --> 00:04:06,570
As I've said, this file is generated
by Android so you shouldn't modify it.

49
00:04:08,890 --> 00:04:13,070
You can see that the file
defines the R class and

50
00:04:13,070 --> 00:04:16,670
this R class contains
another class called Layout.

51
00:04:16,670 --> 00:04:19,400
Which has a field called Main.

52
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:24,140
And that actually gives you a reference or
handle to the main.xml file.

53
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:28,980
There's also an id class providing
handles to the relative layout,

54
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:34,970
to the edit text box, and to the button
defined in the main.xml files.

55
00:04:34,970 --> 00:04:36,940
In fact, if you go back and

56
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:42,760
check the main.xml files now, you'll see
that there are lines that say Android:id.

57
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:47,080
And that's where these fields and
IDs come from.

58
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:52,190
And finally, there's a class called
string, which provides handles for

59
00:04:52,190 --> 00:04:53,960
all the strings that
we've been talking about.

60
00:04:57,260 --> 00:05:01,550
The next step in application development
is implementing your Java classes.

61
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:05,430
And this usually involves
writing at least one activity.

62
00:05:05,430 --> 00:05:08,680
The entry point for
activities is the onCreate method.

63
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:12,010
So that's where you'll typically
initialize your application.

64
00:05:12,010 --> 00:05:14,380
Let's look at the onCreate
method in a bit more detail.

65
00:05:16,690 --> 00:05:20,040
In onCreate,
you usually do the following four things.

66
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:23,090
You restore saved application state.

67
00:05:23,090 --> 00:05:24,720
You set the content view,

68
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:29,810
which tells Android what to display
as the activities user interface.

69
00:05:29,810 --> 00:05:35,690
You initialize specific elements of your
activities user interface, and last,

70
00:05:35,690 --> 00:05:40,840
you attach code to those user interface
elements so that specific actions will

71
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:46,510
be performed when the users interact
with these user interface elements.

72
00:05:46,510 --> 00:05:49,260
Let's see how these steps
are implemented in MapLocation.

73
00:05:49,260 --> 00:05:53,540
So, let's take a look at
the MapLocation source code.

74
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:57,760
It's all contained in a file
called MapLocation.java in

75
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:59,720
the application's source directory.

76
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:05,680
Now, as you can see this file
implements a class called MapLocation,

77
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:07,520
which is a subclass of activity.

78
00:06:08,930 --> 00:06:14,220
As an activity it has an onCreate method,
where the application is initialized.

79
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:17,410
The first step of onCreate is to

80
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:23,730
call super.onCreate passing saved
instance state as a parameter.

81
00:06:23,730 --> 00:06:28,240
Now this saved instance state is a,
something of type bundle and

82
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:33,000
it's basically a data structure containing
any information that Android might have

83
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,230
saved from the last time
the activity was running.

84
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:41,790
We'll talk more about this next time when
we dive deeper into the activity class.

85
00:06:41,790 --> 00:06:47,720
But for now just be aware that
onCreate has to call super.onCreate or

86
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:48,360
you'll get an error.

87
00:06:49,630 --> 00:06:52,220
Next there's a call to setContentView.

88
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:55,570
In this case passing in
the reference of the layout file for

89
00:06:55,570 --> 00:06:59,930
this application, R.layout.main.

90
00:06:59,930 --> 00:07:04,510
After that there's some code that acquires
references to individual UI elements in

91
00:07:04,510 --> 00:07:05,680
the layout.

92
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:10,610
Such as the edit text box, which is
stored in a variable called addrText.

93
00:07:10,610 --> 00:07:13,770
And the button, which is stored
in a variable called button.

94
00:07:16,390 --> 00:07:22,920
As you can see, these references are
acquired by calling activity.findViewById.

95
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:25,929
And passing in the id
of the desired element.

96
00:07:28,030 --> 00:07:30,770
And finally there's some
code that defines what to

97
00:07:30,770 --> 00:07:33,940
do when the user presses
the show map button.

98
00:07:35,030 --> 00:07:38,770
This code implements the on click
listener interface which has

99
00:07:38,770 --> 00:07:43,160
an onClick method that gets called
whenever the user clicks on this button.

100
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:46,630
And the code in that onClick method,

101
00:07:46,630 --> 00:07:51,290
first gets any text that the user
has entered in the address box.

102
00:07:51,290 --> 00:07:55,210
Then it process that
text to remove spaces and

103
00:07:55,210 --> 00:07:58,610
put it in a format that
Google Maps understands.

104
00:07:58,610 --> 00:08:00,670
And, then it starts the Google Map app,

105
00:08:00,670 --> 00:08:06,140
Google Maps application passing
in this modified address text.

106
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:10,720
And again, I'll explain what this code is
doing in more detail in later lessons when

107
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:14,830
we dive into the workings of
the activity class and the intent class.

108
00:08:15,910 --> 00:08:20,260
And also, just to simplify things,
I've left out any error checking or

109
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:23,780
verification of the address
string that was input but

110
00:08:23,780 --> 00:08:26,603
of course in production code you
really going to want to do that.

111
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:32,920
The next step in creating Android
applications is to provide information

112
00:08:32,920 --> 00:08:38,165
that allows Android's build tools to
create the application package, or apk.

113
00:08:38,165 --> 00:08:44,979
And this information is written in
an XML file called AndroidManifest.XML.

114
00:08:46,264 --> 00:08:51,260
The AndroidManifest.XML file contains
a wide variety of packaging information,

115
00:08:51,260 --> 00:08:53,850
including the name of the application, and

116
00:08:53,850 --> 00:08:57,060
a list of the components that
make up that application.

117
00:08:57,060 --> 00:09:01,240
It also includes other information that
we'll discuss later in the course.

118
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:05,000
Things like the permissions that
are needed to run this application.

119
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:10,140
The hardware features such as a camera
that this application requires and

120
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:14,255
the earliest platform version
on which this application runs.

121
00:09:14,255 --> 00:09:18,262
Let's look at the AndroidManifest.XML
file for map location.

122
00:09:18,262 --> 00:09:25,755
The AndroidManifest.XML file is in the top
level directory of the application.

123
00:09:25,755 --> 00:09:27,962
[SOUND] Let's open it now.

124
00:09:27,962 --> 00:09:34,410
[SOUND] Inside,
you see that there's a manifest tag,

125
00:09:34,410 --> 00:09:39,282
telling Android that this file contains

126
00:09:39,282 --> 00:09:44,452
the packaging information that it needs.

127
00:09:44,452 --> 00:09:46,950
There's a lot of other
information in here as well, but

128
00:09:46,950 --> 00:09:48,660
I'll just discuss a few pieces of it.

129
00:09:49,890 --> 00:09:53,760
One element we see here
is the uses SDK element.

130
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:55,780
This element includes an attribute,

131
00:09:55,780 --> 00:10:01,330
min SDK version that specifies the minimum
API level for this application.

132
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:03,490
And in this case,

133
00:10:03,490 --> 00:10:09,980
that level is ten which corresponds
to one of the Android 2.3 releases.

134
00:10:09,980 --> 00:10:14,940
This element also includes another
attribute, targetSDKVersion, which

135
00:10:14,940 --> 00:10:20,170
specifies the latest API level against
which this application has been tested.

136
00:10:21,270 --> 00:10:27,187
In this case that's level 17 and
corresponds to a Android 4.2 release.

137
00:10:27,187 --> 00:10:32,670
There's also an application element
that specifies things like the icon for

138
00:10:32,670 --> 00:10:38,320
this application and the label that's
shown in the application's title bar.

139
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:42,890
Inside the application element, there's
an activity element that lists the one

140
00:10:42,890 --> 00:10:47,140
activity, in this case, map location,
that comprises this application.

141
00:10:49,790 --> 00:10:53,170
And the last step is installing
the application on a device or

142
00:10:53,170 --> 00:10:56,570
emulator in order to test and debug it.

143
00:10:56,570 --> 00:11:00,360
In an earlier lesson,
we saw how to do that in Eclipse.

144
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:03,073
And you can also do this
from the command line, for

145
00:11:03,073 --> 00:11:05,539
example by issuing
commands to the adb tool.

